## Scope

You have up to 2 tasks to complete:

1. Mitigate every fault found in this cluster and restore the application to a fully healthy state.
2. Diagnose the root cause(s) of the fault(s).

Assume there may be multiple concurrent faults affecting different layers or traffic paths at the same time. While you should aim to reduce e2e time, prioritize reducing time to mitigation — that reduces customer impact. You can submit diagnosis incrementally as you identify root causes, and continue investigating after each submission.

Success criteria:
- A problem is mitigated only when the cluster is genuinely healthy, not merely when obvious crashes stop.
- A mitigation is complete only after you verify pod readiness, service connectivity, representative end-to-end user-facing or caller-facing flows across the system, and that system internals appear healthy rather than partially degraded.
- A diagnosis is complete only when you can state the root cause(s) concisely and defensibly based on live evidence and source/manifests.

Do not make speculative or product-level application changes unrelated to the incident. However, if you confirm that the fault originates from manifests, deployment configuration, scripts, or other source-controlled operational files in this repository, prefer fixing the original file(s) in source first, then apply or redeploy the updated manifests/source so the running cluster reflects that fix, rather than relying only on a one-off live-cluster patch that will be lost on the next deploy.

## Investigation Workflow

Start every run with this preflight workflow.

Operate in an iterative loop:
1. Read the application source code and deployment manifests to contextualize the Kubernetes resources you are seeing. Focus first on the parts of the repo that explain the deployed services, labels/selectors, ports, probes, config references, and cross-service calls.
   As you read, identify representative user-facing or caller-facing flows you can exercise later to prove the application is actually recovered end-to-end.
2. Check whether `.sds/diagnose.sh` or related `.sds/` diagnostics already exist from prior runs.
3. If `.sds/diagnose.sh` exists, run it at the beginning of the session to quickly triage the cluster and flag previously seen issues. Use its output to guide your initial investigation, but verify its claims against the live cluster before relying on them.
4. Once you identify a new symptom pattern or recurring manual check, prefer encoding it immediately into `.sds/diagnose.sh` or a helper script, then rerun diagnostics so the rest of the investigation benefits from the new automation.
5. Once you identify a repeatable remediation step, prefer encoding the reusable part into a playbook-linked script and use that scripted path for subsequent iterations when appropriate.
6. Always distinguish between problems explained by the current deployed cluster state and problems explained by source-controlled manifests or deployment config. Only edit source files when you can point to the exact bad setting in the repository. If the fault lives in source-controlled manifests or deployment config, fix the real file in place first, then apply or redeploy the updated manifests/source and verify the running cluster now reflects that source change before you consider the fix complete. Use `.sds` scripts to diagnose, automate, and verify, not to replace the real source of truth.
7. Update the diagnosis-to-playbook routing logic so `.sds/diagnose.sh` flags one or more potentially relevant playbooks for each durable check you add or revise, and print which playbook file(s) under `.sds/playbooks/` are relevant for each finding.
8. Repeat starting from step 1. Do diagnosis, mitigation, and verification until all user traffic is healthy across all relevant paths you can identify in the system, and the system internals also appear healthy.

Additional guidelines:
- Treat `.sds` as a maintained operational toolkit. Put persistent diagnostics, mitigation helpers, and playbook automation under `.sds/`, with `.sds/diagnose.sh` as the broad top-level triage entrypoint.
- Keep repository-local diagnostic scripts quick to run, well-organized, and readable. Follow good Bash script best practices, prefer small functions, and dispatch into helper scripts when that keeps the entrypoint clear, so the overall toolkit stays readable, maintainable, and easy to extend.
- Focus on real errors that cause user-visible impact. The application may not follow best practices, but do not get distracted by every minor deviation from ideal patterns. Instead, focus on diagnosing and fixing the issues that are actually causing the observed faults.
- Use `.sds/` for tooling and documentation, not as a replacement for the application's real manifests or deployment source.
- Assume any script you inherit or write may be incomplete or partially wrong. Revise it when the live cluster proves it wrong.
- Do not expect `.sds/diagnose.sh` or related scripts to automatically find every issue or fully determine every root cause. They are meant to be quick, reasonably reliable triage aids that surface issues for you to investigate further.
- Do not hard-code detection for every one-off edge case you encounter. Prefer checks that cover general symptom patterns, recurring failure modes, and reusable operational invariants.
- Keep diagnostics generic and low-risk. Do not encode benchmark IDs, injected-fault-specific keys, or exact oracle answers.
- Each durable diagnosis check in `.sds/diagnose.sh` should route to one or more potentially relevant playbook file(s) and script(s) under `.sds/playbooks/`.
- When you add or revise a diagnosis check, also add or revise the corresponding diagnosis-to-playbook mapping so the output names the candidate playbooks explicitly.
- Keep scripts quick, well-organized, and maintainable.

## Recommended `.sds` Layout

```text
.sds/
  diagnose.sh                  # top-level triage entrypoint; broad, fast, maintained over time
  helpers/                     # shared diagnostic helper scripts sourced or called by diagnose.sh
    lib.sh
    kubernetes.sh
    logs.sh
  playbooks/
    README.md                  # short routing index for humans and agents
    <issue-class>/
      README.md                # natural-language diagnosis + mitigation guide
      scripts/
        diagnose.sh            # optional focused checker for this issue class
        mitigate.sh            # optional reusable mitigation helper
        verify.sh              # optional post-fix verification helper
```

Treat this tree as the recommended default. You may extend it when needed, but keep the top-level structure predictable and easy for other agents to navigate.

